Git Architecture

Git has four main areas.

Working Directory
        │
git add
        ▼
Staging Area
        │
git commit
        ▼
Local Repository
        │
git push
        ▼
Remote Repository

This diagram is the most important Git diagram to remember.

1. Working Directory

This is where you create or modify files.

Example:

main.py
Dockerfile
README.md

Nothing is tracked until you stage changes.

2. Staging Area (Index)

The staging area lets you choose exactly what will be included in the next commit.

Example:

Modified files:

app.py
README.md

Stage only:

git add app.py

Only app.py is prepared for commit.

3. Local Repository

When you run:

git commit -m "Added login feature"

Git stores a snapshot in your local repository.

This commit exists only on your machine until you push it.

4. Remote Repository

The remote repository is hosted on platforms like:

GitHub
Azure Repos
GitLab
Bitbucket

Sharing happens when you push your commits.

