Git Objects

Internally, Git stores four object types.

Object	Purpose
Blob	File contents
Tree	Directory structure
Commit	Snapshot with metadata
Tag	Named reference to a commit
Blob

Stores file contents.

Example:

README.md

↓

Blob Object

Tree

Represents folders.

project/

README.md

Dockerfile

src/

The tree links files and directories together.

Commit

A commit stores:

Author
Timestamp
Commit message
Parent commit
Tree reference

Example:

Added Login API

Each commit represents a snapshot of the project.

Tag

A tag marks an important commit.

Example:

v1.0

v2.0

v3.0

Used for releases.

SHA-1 Hash

Every Git object has a unique hash.

Example:

c9b1d7e8f234...

Git uses these hashes to identify commits and objects.

Benefits:

Integrity
Uniqueness
Easy referencing
